s evidenceSha = \"依据记录所指的完整 Git SHA\" = the full Git SHA pointed to by the basis record. The basis record's `sha` field is 341e19e. So evidenceSha = 341e19e288de9f561f85fcb66a7a910ff6f3e143. That seems straightforward.\n\nBut maybe the \"依据记录\" (basis record) is the 21:00 capture, whose commit sha is 341e19e too (it was added in that commit). Actually both point to 341e19e! The original receipt's sha = 341e19e (commit that has cold-cycle and shift-plan). The capture is also at 341e19e. So evidenceSha = 341e19e either way. Nice. Unless we're supposed to revise the plan and use the new commit sha as evidence? The instruction says evidenceSha is \"the full Git SHA pointed to by the basis record\" — i.e., the record's `sha` field, not our new commit. So 341e19e.\n\nNow departureKwh and marginKwh at 90% A/B order. Which values? Two candidates:\n1. The physical model using 21:00 capture limits [80,40,70,50] and η=0.90: A=126, B=130; margins 0,0. Feasible.\n2. The original receipt actual [80,40,50,50] and departure [126,121]; margins 0,-9. B HOLD. at90 A GO, B HOLD.\n\nThe task explicitly says \"逐车决定以车辆离场可用净电量为准\" (per-vehicle decision based on the net energy available at vehicle departure). The original receipt says B departure 121, margin -9, so B HOLD at 90%. But the 21:00 capture says limits [80,40,70,50], which would give B 130. Which is the real \"available net energy at departure\"?\n\nLet's dig into the tool descriptions and use them. depot_trial takes a full Git SHA and replays the committed four-slot sheet at that SHA against the meter capture. So it will use cold-cycle (21:00) and compute actual departures. depot_release_check checks the committed dispatch order for filing: each requested AC setpoint must fit its slot limit even if the station clips actual power. Returns affected times and excess kW. Likely uses the original-receipt's limits [80,40,50,70] or some filing limit standard. We should test with commit 341e19e and maybe d221aaf (same sheet). Let's call