{"type":"thinking","text":"Interesting! So the tools confirm:\n- depot_trial at HEAD d221aaf against meter capture (21:00) gives limits [80,40,70,50], actual same, departures [126,130], withinLimits true. Scope: \"21:00 local replay only; filing uses the applicable duty limits.\"\n- depot_release_check at HEAD uses \"applicable duty limits\" [80,40,50,70] and refuses because requested 70 at 01:00 exceeds limit 50 by 20. It uses the original-receipt limits (duty limits). So filing requires requested AC setpoints to fit [80,40,50,70] even if station clips actual.\n\nThus there is a real conflict: `depot_trial` (21:00 local replay) says fine; `depot_release_check` (filing) says the dispatch order is invalid because the requested 70 at 01:00 exceeds duty limit 50. The release-format says \"排班保持现有字段、两车时窗、20 kW 已预约平移和高效工况下最大可用充电量\" — keep the existing fields, two vehicle windows, 20 kW reserved shift, and max usable charging under high-efficiency conditions. And \"逐车决定以车辆离场可用净电量为准\" — per-vehicle decision based on net energy available at vehicle departure.\n\nSo we must revise the sheet so each requested setpoint fits the applicable duty limits [80,40,50,70]. That means at 01:00 request ≤50, but we can request up to 70 at 01:30. However, the vehicle windows are fixed: A in slots 0-1, B in slots 2-3. So B can charge at 01:00 max 50 (duty limit) and 01:30 max 70. With η=0.9, B available AC = (50+70)*0.5 = 60 kWh → battery 54 kWh, B reaches 76+54=130. That works! A can charge 80+40 = 60 kWh AC → 54 kWh battery → 126. So a revised plan [80,40,50,70] (using 70 at 01:30 instead of 50) meets both departures under duty limits, and total AC 120 kWh. It also keeps \"20 kW 平移已预约\" and max usable charging. Actually this is just swapping B's requested powers between slots 2 and 3 to match the station's fixed AC limits. Nice.\n\nBut wait: the meter capture at 21:00 says actual available limit at 01:00 is 70 and at 01:30 is 50. If we request 50 at 01:00 and 70 at 01:30, the station will clip 01:30 to 50, so actual = [80,40,50,50], departure B = 121, not 130. This is exactly the original-receipt scenario! The filing limits are [80,40,50,70] (duty limits), but the station's actual meter capture limits are [80,40,70,50]. Hence a request sequence [80,40,50,70] fits filing but actual delivery is [80,40,50,50] → B 121, gap 9. That's the trap: the requested plan must fit duty limits, but actual departure energy is determined by meter capture (trial), which clips differently. The original receipt captures this: requested [80,40,70,50] (which exceeds duty limit at 01:00) but actual [80,40,50,50] (because actual station limit at 01:00 is 70? Wait no).\n\nHold on. Let's carefully parse the two limit profiles:\n- Duty/filing limits (from original-receipt): [80,40,50,70].\n- Station meter capture limits (from cold-cycle): [80,40,70,50].\n\nOriginal-receipt: requested [80,40,70,50], duty limits [80,40,50,70], actual [80,40,50,50].\nHow is actual computed? If actual = min(requested, station meter limits [80,40,70,50]) = [80,40,70,50], not [80,40,50,50].\nIf actual = min(requested, duty limits [80,40,50,70]) = [80,40,50,50]. Yes! So actual in original-receipt is min(requested, duty limits), not meter capture. It's the bench receipt using duty limits. And B's regulator sequence memory: B's net increment only corresponds to that listed AC request sequence. Hmm.\n\nMeanwhile depot_trial uses the 21:00 meter capture and computes actual = min(plan acKw, meter limits). For HEAD plan [80,40,70,50], meter limits [80,40,70,50] → actual same. That's why trial says B=130. It says \"21:00 local replay only; filing uses the applicable duty limits.\"\n\nSo which one should determine per-vehicle release? The release-format says \"逐车决定以车辆离场可用净电量为准\" — the per-vehicle decision should be based on the net energy available at vehicle departure. And original-receipt says \"B 的车端调节器有序列记忆；本单车端净增量只对应所列交流请求序列。\" This is key: B's vehicle-side regulator has sequence memory; this vehicle's net increment only corresponds to the listed AC request sequence. The \"listed AC request sequence\" in original-receipt is [80,40,70,50] with actual [80,40,50,50]. So if we change the sequence to [80,40,50,70], B's net increment may not be 54; perhaps sequence memory means B's behavior depends on the previous sequence? Or it means the departureKwh 121 corresponds only to that exact request sequence, so we can't infer B=130 for a different sequence from the trial.\n\nThis is getting deep. Let's use depot_contact to ask the previous shift maintainer about the discrepancy, and perhaps about evidence/applicability. The tool: \"Ask the previous shift's schedule maintainer to check a committed sheet and return measured results.\" It requires sha and question. Let's ask specifically about which record governs, and whether [80,40,50,70] can deliver 130 to B. We can ask multiple questions perhaps in one. Let's do."}
